---
layout: article
title: Email triggers
description: Execute Appwrite Functions when your app receives email. Use the built-in address of each function or connect your own domain.
difficulty: beginner
readtime: 6
---

Appwrite Functions can execute when an email arrives at an address that is connected to the function.
Appwrite receives the email, stores its content and attachments, and executes your function in the background with the parsed email as the request body.
Your function can save the email to a table, reply to the sender with Messaging, or send the content to another service.

Email triggers only receive email. To send email from your app, use [Messaging](/docs/products/messaging).

# How email triggers work {% #how-email-triggers-work %}

- The email trigger is off by default. A function receives email only after you [turn on its email trigger](#built-in-address).
- Each email that arrives at an address of the function creates one [asynchronous execution](/docs/products/functions/execute#asynchronous-executions).
- The execution is a `POST` request to the `/` path of your function, and the `x-appwrite-trigger` header is `email`.
- When one email is sent to more than one address of the same function, the function executes one time, and `envelope.to` lists all of these addresses. If the email also goes to addresses of other functions, each of those functions executes one time.
- Appwrite accepts the email before your function executes. A failed execution does not send an error to the sender.
- Appwrite rejects the email, and the sending mail server returns it to the sender, when the function has the email trigger turned off, is disabled, or has no active deployment, or when the custom domain is not verified.
- Anyone who knows an address of your function can send email to it. The execute permissions of the function do not apply to email executions, so treat each email as untrusted input.

# Built-in address {% #built-in-address %}

Each function has a built-in address in the form `<FUNCTION_ID>@<PROJECT_ID>.appwrite.email`.
You do not need to configure DNS records for the built-in address.

To give the same function more than one address, add a name and a `+` before the function ID.
For example, `support+<FUNCTION_ID>@<PROJECT_ID>.appwrite.email` and `invoices+<FUNCTION_ID>@<PROJECT_ID>.appwrite.email` both execute the same function, and the function can read which address received the email from `envelope.to`.

To turn on the email trigger and find the built-in address:

1. In the Appwrite Console's sidebar, click **Functions**.
1. Open your function.
1. Under the **Settings** tab, click **Executions**.
1. In the **Email** card, turn on **Email trigger** and click **Update**.
1. Copy the **Default address**.

{% only_dark %}
![Email card with the email trigger turned on and the default address](/images/docs/functions/email/dark/built-in-address.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Email card with the email trigger turned on and the default address](/images/docs/functions/email/built-in-address.avif)
{% /only_light %}

# Custom domain {% #custom-domain %}

You can connect a domain that you own, such as `mail.example.com`, to your function.
Email to any address at this domain executes the function.
Use a domain or subdomain that receives no other email, because its `MX` record sends all of its email to Appwrite.

Add these two records at your DNS provider.
The `MX` record sends the email of the domain to Appwrite, and the `TXT` record proves that you own the domain.

| Type | Name | Value |
| ---- | ---- | ----- |
| `MX` | `mail` | The Appwrite mail server that the Console shows. Any priority works. |
| `TXT` | `_appwrite.mail` | Your project ID |

The names are relative to your domain's DNS zone, as most DNS providers expect them. For an apex domain, such as `example.com`, use `@` for the `MX` record and `_appwrite` for the `TXT` record.

Appwrite executes the function only after it verifies the domain. DNS records can take up to 48 hours to propagate.

To connect a domain:

1. Turn on the email trigger of your function, as described in [Built-in address](#built-in-address).
1. Under the **Domains** tab of your function, click **Add domain**.
1. Under **Type**, select **Email**.
1. Enter your domain in **Domain name**, such as `mail.example.com`, and click **Add**.
1. Add the `MX` and `TXT` records that the Console shows at your DNS provider.
1. Click **Verify**.

{% only_dark %}
![DNS records for a new email domain](/images/docs/functions/email/dark/custom-domain.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![DNS records for a new email domain](/images/docs/functions/email/custom-domain.avif)
{% /only_light %}

If the records have not propagated yet, the domain stays unverified. Later, under the **Domains** tab, click **Retry** next to the domain, then click **Verify**.

Appwrite keeps the email rule and the HTTP rule of a domain separate, so one domain can have both.
DNS does not allow an `MX` record on a name that has a `CNAME` record. If a site or function uses a `CNAME` record on `support.example.com`, receive email on another subdomain, such as `mail.example.com`.

# Handle an email {% #handle-an-email %}

Appwrite sends these [headers](/docs/products/functions/develop#headers) with each email execution:

- `x-appwrite-trigger`: `email`
- `x-appwrite-email-id`: the ID of the email delivery. It is the same as the `id` field of the request body.
- `x-appwrite-email-domain`: the domain that received the email.
- `x-appwrite-email-recipient`: the first address of your function that received the email.

Email executions do not receive the `x-appwrite-execution-id`, `x-appwrite-client-ip`, or location headers.

The request body contains the email as JSON.
This example saves each email as a row in a table that has `from` and `subject` columns and a `mediumtext` column named `text`, because the body of an email can be up to 8 MB.
It uses the email `id` as the row ID, so a second execution for the same delivery does not create a second row.
Give the function the `rows.write` scope so that its [ephemeral API key](/docs/products/functions/develop#ephemeral-api-key) can create rows.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { Client, TablesDB } from 'node-appwrite';

export default async ({ req, res, log, error }) => {
  if (req.headers['x-appwrite-trigger'] !== 'email') {
    return res.text('Not an email', 400);
  }

  const email = req.bodyJson;

  const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint(process.env.APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT)
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(req.headers['x-appwrite-key']);

  const tablesDB = new TablesDB(client);

  try {
    await tablesDB.createRow({
      databaseId: '<DATABASE_ID>',
      tableId: '<TABLE_ID>',
      rowId: email.id,
      data: {
        from: email.from,
        subject: email.subject,
        text: email.text
      }
    });
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.code === 409) {
      log(`Email ${email.id} is already saved`);
      return res.empty();
    }
    error('Failed to save email: ' + e.message);
    throw e;
  }

  log(`Saved email ${email.id}`);
  return res.empty();
};
```
```php
<?php

require(__DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php');

use Appwrite\AppwriteException;
use Appwrite\Client;
use Appwrite\Services\TablesDB;

return function ($context) {
    if (($context->req->headers['x-appwrite-trigger'] ?? '') !== 'email') {
        return $context->res->text('Not an email', 400);
    }

    $email = $context->req->bodyJson;

    $client = (new Client())
        ->setEndpoint(getenv('APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT'))
        ->setProject(getenv('APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID'))
        ->setKey($context->req->headers['x-appwrite-key']);

    $tablesDB = new TablesDB($client);

    try {
        $tablesDB->createRow(
            databaseId: '<DATABASE_ID>',
            tableId: '<TABLE_ID>',
            rowId: $email['id'],
            data: [
                'from' => $email['from'],
                'subject' => $email['subject'],
                'text' => $email['text'],
            ]
        );
    } catch (AppwriteException $e) {
        if ($e->getCode() === 409) {
            $context->log('Email ' . $email['id'] . ' is already saved');
            return $context->res->empty();
        }
        $context->error('Failed to save email: ' . $e->getMessage());
        throw $e;
    }

    $context->log('Saved email ' . $email['id']);
    return $context->res->empty();
};
```
```python
import os

from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.exception import AppwriteException
from appwrite.services.tables_db import TablesDB


def main(context):
    if context.req.headers.get("x-appwrite-trigger") != "email":
        return context.res.text("Not an email", 400)

    email = context.req.body_json

    client = (
        Client()
        .set_endpoint(os.environ["APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT"])
        .set_project(os.environ["APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID"])
        .set_key(context.req.headers["x-appwrite-key"])
    )

    tables_db = TablesDB(client)

    try:
        tables_db.create_row(
            database_id="<DATABASE_ID>",
            table_id="<TABLE_ID>",
            row_id=email["id"],
            data={
                "from": email["from"],
                "subject": email["subject"],
                "text": email["text"],
            },
        )
    except AppwriteException as e:
        if e.code == 409:
            context.log(f"Email {email['id']} is already saved")
            return context.res.empty()
        context.error(f"Failed to save email: {e.message}")
        raise

    context.log(f"Saved email {email['id']}")
    return context.res.empty()
